      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we first need to analyze the shape of the figure and find the area of each sector and the quadrilateral.\n\nThe given problem describes two sectors of a circle with a radius of 12 and an angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) at the center. When two such sectors are placed side by side, they form a quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) with \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) as the sides of the quadrilateral.\n\nEach sector has a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) and a radius of 12. The area of one sector is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{1}{6} \\pi r^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\pi (12)^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\pi \\cdot 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\nSince there are two such sectors, the combined area of the two sectors is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of two sectors} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of the quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\). The quadrilateral can be divided into two right triangles by drawing a line from the center of the circle to the midpoint of \\(AB\\). Each of these right triangles has a hypotenuse of 12 and one angle of \\(60^\\circ\\), making the other angle \\(30^\\circ\\).\n\nThe area of one right triangle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one right triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\n\\]\nSince the base and height of each right triangle are equal, the base and height are both equal to the length of the chord \\(AB\\). To find the length of the chord \\(AB\\), we use the Law of Cosines in \\(\\triangle OAB\\), where \\(O\\) is the center of the circle:\n\\[\nAB^2 = OA^2 + OB^2 - 2 \\cdot OA \\cdot OB \\cdot \\cos(60^\\circ) = 12^2 + 12^2 - 2 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 144 + 144 - 144 = 144\n\\]\nThus, \\(AB = \\sqrt{144} = 12\\). The area of one right triangle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one right triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times 12 \\times \\sin(60^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times 12 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSince there are two such right triangles, the total area of the quadrilateral is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of quadrilateral} = 2 \\times 36\\sqrt{3} = 72\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is the sum of the area of the two sectors and the area of the quadrilateral:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of figure } ABCD = 48\\pi + 72\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi + 72\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
