      "text": "To determine the area of figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to analyze the configuration of the sectors and the resulting shape. Each sector has a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) and a radius of \\(12\\).\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one sector. The formula for the area of a sector of a circle is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of a sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle in degrees and \\(r\\) is the radius. For our sector, \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(r = 12\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\nSince there are two such sectors, the total area of the two sectors is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of two sectors} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the area of the equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\). The side length of the equilateral triangle is equal to the length of the arc connecting the endpoints of the sectors. The arc length of each sector is:\n\\[\n\\text{Arc length} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times 2\\pi \\times 12 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times 24\\pi = 4\\pi\n\\]\nSince the arc length is the same as the side length of the equilateral triangle, the side length of triangle \\(ABC\\) is \\(4\\pi\\).\n\nThe area of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(s = 4\\pi\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of triangle } ABC = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} (4\\pi)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 16\\pi^2 = 4\\sqrt{3}\\pi^2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is the sum of the area of the two sectors and the area of the triangle:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of figure } ABCD = 48\\pi + 4\\sqrt{3}\\pi^2\n\\]\n\nHowever, upon re-evaluating the problem, it appears there was an error in the calculation of the sector area. The correct area of one sector is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{1}{6} \\times 144\\pi = 24\\pi\n\\]\nThus, the total area of the two sectors is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of two sectors} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\nThe area of the equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\) is correct, but it should be calculated as:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of triangle } ABC = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} (4\\sqrt{3})^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 48 = 12\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the correct area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of figure } ABCD = 48\\pi + 12\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi + 12\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
